<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kilwa Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">957 CE – 1513 CE (approx.) · capital Kilwa Kisiwani</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The richest Swahili city-state of the Middle Ages, which controlled the gold trade of southern Africa from its island capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilwa-chronicle">Kilwa Chronicle</a> traditionally dates the sultanate to c. 957, when Ali ibn al-Hasan, a prince said to come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, bought the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani">Kilwa Kisiwani</a> from a local ruler. Archaeology suggests a gradual rise from a fishing and trading settlement, with stone building and coinage appearing by the 11th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kilwa grew rich by taking control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a>, the port for gold from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>. Under the Mahdali dynasty, from the late 13th century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-Hasan ibn Sulaiman built the vast palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husuni-kubwa">Husuni Kubwa</a> and enlarged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kilwa">Great Mosque</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, visiting in 1331, called Kilwa one of the most beautiful cities in the world. Its rulers minted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins and traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, gold and slaves for Indian cloth and Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Dynastic quarrels weakened Kilwa in the 15th century, and in 1505 a Portuguese fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a> sacked the city and built a fort there. Trade collapsed, and the town later fell under Omani rule. Its ruins are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kilwa-sultanate.jpg" alt="Kilwa Sultanate" width="320" height="176"><figcaption>Image: Dekodrak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_used_in_the_east_coast_of_Tanzania_according_to_a_Portuguese_1576_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>957 CE – 1513 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kilwa Kisiwani</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali ibn al-Hasan Shirazi, al-Hasan ibn Sulaiman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swahili</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tanzania">Tanzania</a>, <a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Kilwa, Kilwa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1235&amp;focus=kilwa-sultanate" aria-label="Kilwa Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5771 2513 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kilwa Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6091 2769L4773 1044"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1044h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 2769h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6091 2769h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kilwa Kisiwani. <a href="/atlas?year=1235&amp;focus=kilwa-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

